Let $G$ be a group with identity $e$ and let $K$ be a commutative ring containing a subring $k.$
A $k$-algebra is \textit{strongly $G$-graded} if it admits an additive decomposition
\begin{equation}\label{eq:D:1}
A_G = \bigoplus_{g \in G} A_g
\end{equation}
such that for every $g,h \in G$
\begin{equation}\label{AgAh}A_g\cdot A_h=A_{gh}.\end{equation}

With the above notations
\begin{enumerate}
\item $\tilde{\mbox{Cliff}}(\phi)$ is a monoid under the product \eqref{newgralg}, whose identity is the class $[K_{\phi}G ]$ of the corresponding skew group algebra.
Moreover,
\item the GCC map \eqref{chi} well-defines a homomorphism
\begin{equation}\label{chiphi}\tilde{\chi}_\phi: \tilde{\mbox{Cliff}}(\phi)\to {\mathcal{\tilde{C}}{(\phi)}}
\end{equation}   of monoids.
